in adrian tchaikovsky's children of time  humanity is spreading out into the stars  but not with colonies but with  evolutionary science  we have seeded a number of planets with  apes and a special  virus that will accelerate their  evolution watching from satellites  orbiting the planet above  in no time at all we will have created  planets filled with our own  proto-humans we will be  gods except  it all goes wrong the apes never make it  and the virus doesn't infect them the  virus  infects spiders children of time is one  of the best books i've read in a long  time and it's easily the best sci-fi  book i have perhaps  ever read and i want to tell you why for  starters it has some of the coolest  world building i have  ever seen it takes place over thousands  of years so you're literally watching  the ascendancy of this  sentient spider civilization they use  ant colonies as computers in this book  i mean i know that sounds crazy but the  way that they do it is just so damn cool  tchaikovsky really digs into what would  it be like if spiders were the  dominant species how would they live how  would they think  the spiders have equivalents of the  prisoner's dilemma but they think in  terms of intricate interconnectivity  of a well not just of sight but of  constant vibration and scent  the idea of two prisoners incapable of  communication would not be an acceptable  status quo for them  but a problem to overcome the prisoner's  dilemma is a gordian knot  to be cut through rather than be bound  by  ah it's so good thematically the story  juxtaposes the ascendancy of spider  civilization with the degeneration and  breakdown of human civilization there  are two parallel stories that are  brought crashing together here and it  really asks  what does it mean to be civilized what  does it mean to be human when not only  we forget  part of our humanity but we forget the  knowledge that humanity has collectively  brought together over our evolution  what do we become and what does that  make the spiders  one of the problems with writing stories  that take place over thousands of years  is how do you have consistent character  perspectives but he manages to do that  really well not only with the humans  but with the spiders who go through  generations there are recurring  characters who sort of inherit things  from their parents  which i thought was a really cool world  building mechanic as well also if i  sound sick it's because i am it's making  it immensely hard to record  fundamentally children of time is a  story about empathy it's about  understanding things that are  vastly different from us which of course  spiders are perfect for  because what does the average human fear  or revile  more yet as you read it you come to  visualize these characters which take on  the form of  a thing you might really hate and you  like them you  root for them life is not perfect  individuals will always be flawed but  empathy  the sheer inability to see those around  them as anything other than people too  conquers all in the end tchaikovsky does  this  amazing thing where he literally puts  the reader on the same character arc  as the human characters in the book  itself i love that it's so  meta challenging us to empathize with  these things we would normally  hate and yet even with a story filled  with all of these complicated thematic  ideas  tchaikovsky still manages to write a  story filled with  vibrant characters that we care about a  story that you really get into on a  personal level not just an ideological  one now this isn't me saying that it's a  character-centric story because you  really do fall in love with the worlds  themselves the world of the spiders the  world of the humans which is why the  sense that they're constantly on a  collision course is so  tense and he pulls it off in this  beautiful ending so incredibly well  he he ties all of these thematic and  world building and character threads  together but if you're looking for a  book which is a character study then  maybe children of time isn't that but  that's not a weakness of the book any  more than saying oh there's not enough  action scenes it's just not the type of  book that it is  and i have to point out that the writing  itself is just fantastic  it's mature without being obtuse it's  accessible without being plain it's  insightful without being heavy-handed  when iran kern says humanity is  overrated  there's this deep sense of cynicism that  you go  yeah we kind of are when you read this  book but it's also well  i won't spoil that what criticisms do i  have of children of time  honestly it feels a little bit forced to  give them but  as i said before it's not a book about  character work it still has characters  who are great  and you root for them or you hate them  and they have character arcs and they  change  but it's not a book about character work  it's not  a john green novel it's not american  gods  so if you're looking for that this might  not be for you  but here is why i think it's an amazing  book if you want to get into sci-fi or  if you're already reading sci-fi  it avoids so many of the pitfalls of  modern science fiction  and it avoids the pitfalls of old  science fiction  while keeping the strengths of both it's  a massive scale story while still  being small and it's thematically deep  without being obnoxious and you know  what's even cooler  the sequel children of ruin is also damn  amazing  so you can read that i just drove  through both of these  so absolutely recommend go pick them up  now but that's all for me let me know  down below what you thought of children  of time and  other books that you want me to review  in the meantime stay nerdy  and i'll see you in the future
 

